   $q$Read the paragraph: "Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity. Solar panels can sit on rooftops. Solar panels do not make smoke." Which revision best reduces the repetitive sentence beginnings?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity, solar panels sit on rooftops, solar panels make no smoke.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity. They can sit on rooftops, and they do not make smoke.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity. Solar panels can sit on rooftops, and solar panels do not make smoke.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"keeps-repetition"},{"text":"They turn sunlight into electricity. They can sit on rooftops. They do not make smoke.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unclear-pronoun"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity. They can sit on rooftops, and they do not make smoke.$q$, $q$Naming "Solar panels" once and then using "They" removes the repetition while joining two ideas into one sentence. Starting every sentence with "They" never tells the reader what "they" are.$q$, 'draft', null)
