   $q$A student wrote in a science notebook: "The thing about the magnet is that it kind of pulls on stuff made of iron." Which revision is the most precise and keeps a scientific tone?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The magnet kind of attracts iron stuff.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"vague-hedging"},{"text":"The magnet does something to things made of iron.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"vague-verb"},{"text":"The magnet attracts objects made of iron.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The magnet violently yanks every metal object in the world.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"inaccurate-exaggeration"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The magnet attracts objects made of iron.$q$, $q$"Attracts" is the exact science word for a magnet pulling, and "objects" replaces "stuff." The first choice uses "attracts" but keeps "kind of" and "stuff," so it still sounds vague and casual.$q$, 'draft', null)
